Hi Helen,

Following Krishna's email on the bst-iaz-sql-ppe-geo databases, I've had a look at the last 30 days of metrics and the current configuration. Krishna's proposal is a good one; the databases are clearly oversized. I just wanted to give you visibility of a few extra details so the changes deliver the full saving with no impact on the weekly workloads.

How serverless billing works (for context)
Serverless databases scale automatically between a minimum and maximum vCore setting. We pay for at least the minimum 24/7, even when idle. The maximum is just the ceiling for busy periods and costs nothing unless it's used. So the minimum drives the cost, and the maximum needs to cover the busiest time.
The lowest minimum the portal allows also depends on the maximum (roughly one-eighth of it), e.g. max 32 allows a min of 4 at the lowest, max 8 allows a min of 1.

1. GeoCuratedData (Serverless, max 32 / min 16)
• Billed CPU over 30 days equals 16 vCores running constantly (the minimum setting), roughly £7.5k/month and most of the server's cost.
• CPU is near 0% most of the time, with a weekly peak of ~4 vCores. Data IO and Log IO are 0%.
• Worth noting: of the two options in Krishna's email, max 8 gives headroom for the weekly peak, and lowering the min alongside it (down to 1) is where most of the saving comes from.

2. OSMM (Serverless, max 8 / min 4)
• Billed CPU equals 4 vCores running constantly, roughly £1.9k/month.
• Weekly CPU peak is ~3 vCores. Data IO and Log IO are 0%. 8 connections in 30 days.
• Worth noting: max 4 rather than 2 would cover the weekly peak, with the min lowered to reduce cost.

3. GeoLocators (Premium P2)
• 0% CPU and IO over 30 days, 8 connections, 38 MB of data, max size 1 TB.
• Agree with moving off P2. Given the very low usage, General Purpose Serverless with auto-pause (or a Standard tier) would fit even better than P1.
• Worth noting: in General Purpose, storage is billed on the max size set, not data used, so reducing the 1 TB max size at the same time avoids ~£170/month in storage. Standard tiers below S3 also cap at 250 GB.

4. FMEShared (Premium P2)
• 0% CPU and IO, and zero connections in the last 30 days. 56 MB of data, max size 500 GB.
• Same approach as GeoLocators. It may also be worth confirming whether it's still needed.

Auto-pause
• Agree with Krishna's suggestion. I've checked, and none of the four databases has geo-replication, failover groups, long-term retention or Data Sync configured, so auto-pause can be used on all of them.
• The first connection after a pause takes ~1 minute, so the apps need to handle that.
• GeoCuratedData has connections every few hours, so it would pause less often than the other three.

Other points for visibility
• The cost analysis screenshot shows £12,473.76 for September, slightly higher than the £11,368 in the email.
• If PPE is used for performance testing before releases, it's worth planning around the smaller sizes.

In short, Krishna's right that these databases are significantly oversized, and the saving he's aiming for is achievable, likely more than 60–80%. The details above should help make sure we get the full benefit without impacting the weekly workloads.

